Ladybird asks the DevTools client to inspect an element by sending an
inspectObject event from its console actor, with a grip that carries a
contentDomReference. Gecko’s WebConsoleActor.inspectObject() sends the
same event for the inspect() function that WebExtensions get in
devtools.inspectedWindow.eval(), and the client’s Toolbox handles it in
inspectObjectActor() and viewElementInInspector(), resolving the
reference with the walker’s getNodeActorFromContentDomReference request.
Gecko’s own Inspect context-menu item doesn’t go through the protocol:
DevToolsShim.inspectNode() calls gDevTools.inspectNode() in the parent
process, which resolves its ContentDOMReference itself.

The event’s inspectFromAnnotation is browser-context-menu, the reason
gDevTools.inspectNode() gives for a selection from that menu. It’s kept
exact because the client compares it: The markup view scrolls to a node
selected for that reason, and highlights it briefly.
